// Code-signing configuration for the SenseBridge app target.
//
// This file is committed and deliberately sets no team of its own. The Apple
// Developer team ID is an account identifier tied to one person's membership;
// baking it into `project.pbxproj` publishes it with the repository and makes
// every contributor's first build try to sign against an account they are not
// in.
//
// Put your own team ID in `Signing.local.xcconfig`, which is gitignored:
//
//     DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = YOURTEAMID
//
// Find it with `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`, or in Xcode under
// Settings → Accounts → Manage Certificates. Note it is *not* the identifier in
// parentheses after the certificate name — that is the certificate's ID, not
// the team's. See docs/ENVIRONMENT.md.
//
// The bundle identifier prefix works the same way, and for the same reason:
// `com.sensebridge.app` is registered to this project's Apple Developer team,
// so a contributor building on their own free Apple ID gets "the app
// identifier cannot be registered to your development team" until they pick a
// prefix of their own. Add one alongside your team ID:
//
//     BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX = com.yourname
//
// Every target derives from it (`<prefix>.app`, `<prefix>.SenseBridgeTests`,
// `<prefix>.SenseBridgeUITests`), so one line changes all three. The default
// lives in `project.pbxproj` as `$(BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX:default=com.sensebridge)`,
// which means an unconfigured clone still builds with the original IDs — you
// only need this when signing against your own team. It can also be set for a
// single command: `xcodebuild BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX=com.yourname ...`.
//
// `#include?` is the optional form: a clone without the local file still
// configures and builds, and fails only at the signing step, with Xcode's own
// "requires a development team" message pointing at the real problem.
#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"

// Not signing, but this file is the app target's only `configFiles` entry (see
// ../project.yml) and Xcode permits exactly one per build configuration, so
// every other build setting the app needs has to chain from here. Crash
// reporting is off and unconfigured by default; Sentry.xcconfig explains both.
#include "Sentry.xcconfig"
